# The worker and CI. Copy this file to .env.
#
# `npm run worker` and `npm run publish` load it with
# `node --env-file-if-exists=.env`; nothing loads it implicitly.
#
# The dashboard reads its own file — see apps/web/.env.example. Everything
# reaches the database through the Supabase API: there is no Postgres
# connection string, and no encryption key either, because repository tokens go
# into Supabase Vault. The app holds no secret of its own.

# The service role key bypasses RLS entirely. It belongs to the analysis worker
# and to `code-quality-import` in CI — never to a browser, and never to the
# dashboard process. Settings -> API -> service_role.
SUPABASE_URL="https://<project-ref>.supabase.co"
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=""

# How often the worker asks for work when the queue is empty. Default 5000.
# CODE_QUALITY_POLL_MS="5000"

# Where the worker clones repositories. Defaults to a folder in the system temp dir.
# CODE_QUALITY_WORKSPACE="C:\Users\you\.code-quality\repos"

# Only for `code-quality-import`, when --org is not passed.
# CODE_QUALITY_ORG=

# The GitHub App, so private repositories clone without a pasted token.
#
# Optional. With both unset the worker still runs and still clones anything a
# stored token reaches — it just says so at startup. With them set, any
# organisation that has installed the app clones with a short-lived
# installation token instead, scoped to the repositories they selected.
#
# These are OUR credential, not a customer's, which is why they live here beside
# the service role key rather than in Supabase Vault. They must never appear in
# apps/web/.env and never under a NEXT_PUBLIC_ name: the private key signs as
# the app for every installation it has.
#
# The private key is the .pem GitHub downloads when you generate one. A PEM is
# multi-line and a dotenv value is not, so either quote it and write the
# newlines as the two characters backslash-n, or keep it on one line the same
# way — the worker puts them back before signing.
# GITHUB_APP_ID=
# GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY=

# Where the "Details" link on a pull request's check run points. Unset just
# omits the link: the worker cannot know the dashboard's public URL, and
# guessing sends people somewhere that does not exist.
# CODE_QUALITY_DASHBOARD_URL=https://code-quality.example.com"<organisation-slug>"
